= June 2024 — The first appointment is no longer private debt

Clinics begin accepting patients under the restored public IVF programme.
Couples who had been pricing loans and another year of waiting now arrive with
a funded referral. Robert Biedroń, New Left co-chair and a long-time equality
campaigner, wants voters to remember which part of the coalition kept the
promise alive.

[? if left_in_government: Our ministers may claim delivery, share it with the
partners who passed the budget or spend cabinet capacity on access outside the
largest cities.?][? if not left_in_government: The cabinet and Health Ministry
implemented the programme. Lewica may claim its pressure, share credit or fund
an expansion campaign; it cannot tell patients that opposition opened the
clinic.?]

- @ivf_claim
- @ivf_share
- @ivf_expand

@ivf_claim
title: Make IVF a recognisable Left delivery
subtitle: Put Biedroń and Lewica beside the clinic doors; coalition partners will dispute who turned the promise into a funded service.
on-arrival: left_poll_momentum += 0.25; media_capacity += 2; ko_relation -= 2; progressive_credibility += 3; news_headline = "Lewica puts its name on the IVF programme"

Biedroń visits clinics with patients and local organisers while party material
reprints the years in which the Left defended public funding. The reform gains
a political author people can recognise.

KO answers with the budget, the ministry and the signatures required to open
the programme. Patients still receive treatment; the coalition begins arguing
over whether delivery belongs to the advocate or the office which executed it.

- @poland_event_queue: Put the Left claim on the events desk

@ivf_share
title: Present it as proof that the coalition can deliver together
subtitle: Give every governing partner a place beside the programme; trade a distinct Left achievement for trust in joint government.
